{
  "dom_id": "step-38196",
  "name": "The Rule of the Lazy Statistician for Two Continuous Random Variables",
  "problem": "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables states that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\big[r(X,Y)] = \\iint_{\\mathbb{R}^2} r(x,y) \\, f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}x \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nThe rule of the lazy statistician for two random variables is especially useful for continuous random variables since computing the PDF of $W = r(X, Y)$ can be challenging.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
